00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:46.590
Now that that they got insurance, well
that's gone.

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.430
So in, especially in latter half of the
20th century,

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:53.586
there was a huge explosion of medical
knowledge in technology.

00:06:53.586 --> 00:06:59.580
Exemplified, for example, by the fact that
in the year 2000 25,000 articles

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:08.750
were published reporting randomized
controlled trials that's around the world.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:16.900
Just a huge number and so the second half
of the 20th

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:22.100
century there was this big struggle to
introduce spending restraint, you know?

00:07:23.605 --> 00:07:29.366
Healthcare shot up from 5% of the GDP to
18% and how can we slow that down?

00:07:29.366 --> 00:07:37.700
So the consequences of all of that for the
20th century were, first it cost too much.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.920
Like, 18% of the GDP, straining public and
private finances.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.660
A lot of bad quality care.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:51.190
You know, people think oh, well, we got
the best healthcare system in the world.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:54.620
That's not, obviously, if you look at it
up close.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.760
I was reading an article the other day,

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:01.270
research indicating that something like
400,000 deaths happened in

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:06.500
hospitals per year because of errors and,
preventable errors.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.390
We got to where there was poor access,
nearly 50

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:16.650
million people without health insurance
because it was kinda priced out of reach.

00:08:18.061 --> 00:08:22.900
And employers, and Medicare, essentially
locked in their employees, or

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:27.830
their beneficiaries into what I call
open-ended-fee-for-service.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:31.780
That is the traditional model of paying
for doctors.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:33.310
And most people didn't even have a choice.

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:35.330
That is most employed people, they come to
work, and

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:40.450
the employer says, well this is our health
plan and that's it.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:46.350
Instead of doing, let's say, what we do at
Stanford for our employees on healthcare,

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:49.770
is we say well, we're gonna offer you a
menu of five or

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:52.010
six different health plans that you might
pick.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.030
And we will pay for the, the university
will pay for

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:57.660
the low price plan which around here is
usually Kaiser Permanente.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:59.030
We think that's very good quality,

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:05.090
if you want something that costs more, you
pay the difference.

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:11.308
That's the basic idea of managed
competition.

00:09:11.308 --> 00:09:15.680
so, more consequences in the 20th century,
a huge amount of waste.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.715
A National Academy of Sciences report
estimated some 30 to

00:09:18.715 --> 00:09:22.220
40% of healthcare spending in this country
is waste.

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:27.280
That is, it just does not benefit the
patient.

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.050
More and more consciousness of the fact
that we have an extremely complex

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:37.570
system, just wildly complex in, in, in
it's many aspects.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:43.356
You just think there are thousands of
different insurance plan designs,

00:09:43.356 --> 00:09:47.400
and different employers, so forth.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.380
And so, think of the poor Palo Alto
Clinic, they've,

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:54.560
these people with huge number of different
health plan designs.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:00.150
So they've gotta figure out now for each
person, what is it that the insurance

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:03.460
pays so we can bill the insurance, and
what is it the patient pays?

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:11.680
And so you have very large clerical staffs
in these organizations.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:17.050
And physicians overwhelmed by new medical
information.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:25.075
Just not possible for the ordinary single
doc now, to keep up with it all.

00:10:25.075 --> 00:10:25.980
That's one of our problems.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:28.740
Then, let me just move forward to 21st
century medicine.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:33.762
I think the future is becoming integrated
delivery systems.

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:37.110
That is, with an ethos of, and a culture
of teamwork,

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:39.380
shared responsibility for patients.

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:42.230
Instead of the doc thinking it's my
patient.

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.650
Now a team of doctors from different
specialties,

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:49.000
if it's a complicated patient, need to
work together.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:53.290
It's our patient and we need to share
information.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:58.520
And use our resources as best we can.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:05.320
With integrated delivery systems the
important thing is the incentives

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:10.080
of all the players are aligned with what
Dr, Berwick called the triple aim,

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.548
that is better health, better care, and
lower cost.

00:11:14.548 --> 00:11:21.200
That's, that's where

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.620
we need to move the system.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:27.270
What else with 21st century medicine.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.080
Teams develop up-to-date practice
guidelines.

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:33.770
You know, with the 25,000 new randomized
trials published each year,

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:38.770
you really need a system, a teamwork
system to create useable knowledge.

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:42.230
And to turn the new discoveries into
recommendations for

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:47.222
the physicians for how to take care of
that kind of patient.

00:11:47.222 --> 00:11:53.350
We're moving into standardization, and
simplification.

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:56.250
A focus on chronic conditions.

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:58.770
I mentioned earlier, with 19th century
medicine, it was, it was acute care.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:04.480
Now, about three quarters of the costs

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:08.550
of healthcare are for people with chronic
conditions.

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:12.120
With diabetes, congestive heart failure,
et cetera, et cetera, depression.

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:16.890
And a very promising development, in my
view,

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:19.780
that's starting to come into play now,

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:25.280
is some of the large benefits consulting
firms have created a benefits exchanges.

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:30.520
Aon, Towers, Mercer have done this.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:35.830
So they contract, let's see Aon contracted
with Walgreen's that they will put before

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:44.520
each patient, each worker, a menu of maybe
five or six different choices.

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:49.670
And the employer will make a fixed defined
contribution,

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:52.400
and like Stanford then the employee will
make his choice.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:57.880
And I think that's just crucial, to me
that's the highest priority thing is just

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:02.900
breaking out of the, of the situation
where people don't have choices and

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:05.270
into a kind of market, market model.

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:09.860
And that's, that's starting to happen now.

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:14.911
Okay, that brings us to the Accountable
Care Act, Affordable Care Act,

00:13:14.911 --> 00:13:17.654
which happened in about 2010.

00:13:17.654 --> 00:13:20.968
A good thing there are exchanges which
we've all

00:13:20.968 --> 00:13:25.985
heard about because the implementation was
so grievously blotched.

00:13:25.985 --> 00:13:30.740
But for people who don't have employers to
broker,

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:39.000
to arrange their health insurance they can
go to the exchange.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:40.150
Now, I think exchanges, as I was saying
earlier,

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:42.830
are a very good and important and
necessary idea.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:46.975
My regret that I expressed to the
Obama-nauts was,

00:13:46.975 --> 00:13:51.740
that ought to cover everybody, not just
people who don't have an employer.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:56.830
At least we ought to have every firm up
to, say, 200 employees.

00:13:58.280 --> 00:13:59.605
I sometimes wonder, why not?

00:13:59.605 --> 00:14:04.080
[LAUGH] I mean that's the American way, to
give people responsible choices.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:07.000
Anyway, so that's,

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:10.370
that's a prominent well known feature of
the of the Affordable Care Act.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:16.850
Also, other features, they enacted what's
called the Cadillac tax,

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:24.220
an excised tax of 40% on the excess

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:28.810
of family premiums, over $27,500 a year.

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:34.110
That sounds wild but I mean, normal
projections isn't where things are going,

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:38.440
is we'll be there by 2018, when this thing
kicks in.

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:42.190
And a lot of employers are starting to
work hard, including Stanford.

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:47.343
How can we avoid that tax, how can we keep
the premiums down?

00:14:47.343 --> 00:14:52.427
They also, to pay for the expanding
coverage, they reduced Medicare

00:14:52.427 --> 00:14:57.766
hospital patients, essentially, payments,
essentially with Medicare.

00:14:57.766 --> 00:15:02.480
The government was saying each year we'll
increase your payments by the amount of

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:06.056
inflation and the cost of doing hospital
business plus 1%.

00:15:06.056 --> 00:15:09.696
Well they took the 1% away.

00:15:09.696 --> 00:15:11.140
Probably fine.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:15.790
They created what are call, called
accountable care organizations Auh,

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:21.790
to modify the incentives for doctors and
hospitals caring for Medicare patients.

00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:25.220
The idea with the Accountable Care
Organization is, again, to

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:29.990
bring some teamwork and com, cooperation
into the, into the situation by

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:37.550
doctors and hospitals accepting
responsibility for

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:41.470
moderating the growth in per-person
expenses.

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:48.135
And if they can hold the cost to less than
the projections,

00:15:48.135 --> 00:15:51.800
then they get half the savings.

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:53.380
Now I've been rather skeptical of that

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:56.030
because I think the incentives are far too
weak.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:15:59.440
It'd be better if we just had a market,
and they said, here you are, guys.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:03.360
If you don't really, you know, give
patients a good deal,

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:07.180
high quality, good service, and so forth,
you're just gonna lose your business.

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:08.350
Instead, it was,

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:13.860
if you don't slow the growth in spending,
you won't get this bonus.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:20.675
Sometimes the way I put it is, is, there
we are in the board room with the doctors

00:16:20.675 --> 00:16:24.180
and hospitals and someone in the back of
the room who is skeptical says,

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:28.430
You mean to tell me if we cut our revenue
by a million dollars,

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:33.850
then, we'll get to keep, we'll give,
they'll give us back 500,000.

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:35.895
Well, why don't we just keep the whole
million?

00:16:35.895 --> 00:16:39.240
[LAUGH] There's a problem with the
incentives.

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:42.960
I think, I think one of the problems is
that people who

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:46.680
designed this law didn't study economics
very much, at least,

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:48.760
they don't
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:53.170
&gt;&gt; And finally, there was the Independent
Payment Advisory Board

00:16:54.240 --> 00:17:00.520
which was to 15 wise persons we sit back
and if Medicare spending

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:05.360
grows too much, then they could
essentially make recommendations that

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:10.720
would have the force of law, without
checks and balances and so forth.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:12.660
That hasn't happened so far and it
probably won't.

00:17:13.670 --> 00:17:15.650
My guess is nobody's gonna want that job.

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:19.960
Now what have they done?

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:23.660
I've talked about the big problems of
access, cost, quality.

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:26.510
What what does it do for these problems?

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:30.220
Well one good thing is they encourage and

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:33.680
subsidize the adoption of information
technology.

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:40.310
Another is to penalize hospitals for me,
medicare cases for

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:41.680
readmissions within 30 days.

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:46.830
A generally accepted marker of a, of a
poor treatment.

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:49.400
Generally speaking, I would say,

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:53.982
with our very large quality problems, the
Affordable Care Act didn't do much.

00:17:53.982 --> 00:18:00.033
For access to care the act provided
Medicaid for

00:18:00.033 --> 00:18:08.749
individual adults with incomes up to 1.3
times the federal poverty line.

00:18:08.749 --> 00:18:12.902
And for people with incomes higher than
that through the exchanges,

00:18:12.902 --> 00:18:14.621
there would be subsidies for

00:18:14.621 --> 00:18:19.360
people with incomes all the way up to four
times the federal poverty line.

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:27.590
That's, for a family of four, that's like
$94,000, $9,400.

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:29.670
Guaranteed issue, guaranteed renewal.

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:36.010
That is today, if an insurance company is
selling an insurance plan to the public,

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:40.530
they have to take all comers, the sick and
the healthy.

00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:47.920
So guaranteed renewal, you can't take away
the pol, policy when the person gets sick.

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:53.550
And an individual mandate, that is a law
that if you don't have health insurance,

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:57.030
you have to pay what economists call a
free writer tax.

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:01.920
That is, if you don't have health
insurance, you know that when

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:05.850
you get smashed up on the freeway, and you
get taken in the emergency room.

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:09.208
By law, for many years, the hospital has
to take care of you,

00:19:09.208 --> 00:19:12.430
at least stabilize you.

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.470
And so there's been growing concern about
free writing.

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:18.670
I mean a lot of people are, there's a kind
of health insurance there.

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:24.090
But the Affordable Care Act is definitely
reducing the number

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:30.218
of people who are uninsured, from
something like 50 million to 30 million.

00:19:30.218 --> 00:19:34.520
With complexity, I think it just made the
whole problem worse.

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:39.500
I mean, the thing is bewilderingly
complex.

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:41.980
What about fragmentation?

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:43.950
Well, I talked about an accountable PR
organization.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:48.373
The team of physicians and hospitals that
contract their Medicare,

00:19:48.373 --> 00:19:51.585
the slow cost growth while maintaining
quality.

00:19:51.585 --> 00:19:53.787
And then they can keep half the savings.

00:19:53.787 --> 00:19:58.025
I think Accountable Care Organizations are
an important step in the right direction.

00:19:58.025 --> 00:20:05.111
I just regret that that they didn't make
the incentives stronger,

00:20:05.111 --> 00:20:07.280
because they need to be.

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:11.607
It's a step toward an integrated health
care delivery system.

00:20:12.910 --> 00:20:16.730
You hear a lot of complaints about counter
productive taxes.

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:21.990
To pay for all this they imposed a 2.3%
excise tax on drugs and

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:28.250
devices and the device manufacturing
companies complained that we

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:35.273
are starting to pay taxes on the revenues
even before we make any profits on the,

00:20:35.273 --> 00:20:37.520
on the product.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:42.403
And so it's been, I think, justly
criticized as being anti innovation.

00:20:42.403 --> 00:20:49.350
Similarly, there's a tax on, on insurers
that this is kind of an obscure point,

00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:55.740
but nobody likes insurance companies, so
we're gonna make them pay.

00:20:55.740 --> 00:21:02.570
Well, the problem is the tax falls heavier
on insurance companies at their risk.

00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:05.698
Then half of employed people are then
employers who are, or

00:21:05.698 --> 00:21:07.630
the employers self-insured.

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:11.870
There's no insurance company maybe to
process the claims.

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:18.790
Anyway, it's a tax that works against risk
bearing, which I think is a mistake,

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:24.730
because, what we need is to get insurance
companies bearing the risk and

00:21:24.730 --> 00:21:28.330
then to making deals with doctors and
hospitals to share the risk,

00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:32.330
to transfer the risk for the cost of care
to doctors and hospitals.

00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:36.969
And I think the employer mandate is
anti-jobs.

00:21:36.969 --> 00:21:41.790
It's my first reaction when I saw the
whole thing was,

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:44.588
if I was the CEO of a company, I would say
don't hire anybody.

00:21:44.588 --> 00:21:49.300
Wait till, because it takes away the
ability, of course,

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:54.120
all employers, like, 98% of employers of
more than, then 200 people do

00:21:56.500 --> 00:22:01.220
provide health insurance but it takes away
their flexibility,

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:08.703
their ability to modify it to save money,
because it's all under federal regulation.

00:22:08.703 --> 00:22:12.650
So, summary comments.

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:14.640
Getting everyone covered is an important
goal.

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:16.530
I believe in that.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:21.720
I really think it's wrong to, to have a
situation in which many

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:27.063
people don't have access to healthcare
because, especially children,

00:22:27.063 --> 00:22:30.050
because they, they can't afford it.

00:22:30.050 --> 00:22:34.030
And we really ought to have universal
health insurance.

00:22:34.030 --> 00:22:38.780
And the Affordable Care Act is a serious
attempt to get there.

00:22:38.780 --> 00:22:44.150
I hear some people criticize it and say we
want to repeal it and replace it,

00:22:44.150 --> 00:22:49.310
and now that I am listening, do I hear
what the replacement is going to be?

00:22:49.310 --> 00:22:54.260
And that's why, you know I have some
friends who want to, you know,

00:22:54.260 --> 00:22:58.610
I'd like to hear little more about I mean,
the Republicans,

00:22:58.610 --> 00:23:03.330
I think, could, could improve their whole
story if they would get together and

00:23:03.330 --> 00:23:05.980
come out with a credible and coherent
alternative.

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:11.450
I think that the whole thing is far too
complex, and it keeps changing.

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:16.590
Nancy Pelosi famously said along the way,

00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:20.755
we're going to have to pass this law in
order to find out what's in it.

00:23:20.755 --> 00:23:25.606
[LAUGH] And, and I'm pretty good friends
with our benefits manager, who,

00:23:25.606 --> 00:23:30.303
because I am, I chair the advisory
committee and from time to time we

00:23:30.303 --> 00:23:35.251
say well, four years have gone by and we
still don't know what's in it.

00:23:35.251 --> 00:23:37.978
You know, the president is changing it all
the time.

00:23:37.978 --> 00:23:40.999
Slipping this deadline and that.

00:23:40.999 --> 00:23:45.921
And I think that there's some serious over
reach.

00:23:45.921 --> 00:23:51.521
I think we could of had universal health
insurance in this country without passing

00:23:51.521 --> 00:23:56.796
a law that forces Georgetown University
and Notre Dame University for,

00:23:56.796 --> 00:24:02.790
as employers for example to provide what
they consider to be abortifacients.

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:06.050
That is, abortion causing drugs.

00:24:06.050 --> 00:24:11.920
We just didn't have to take that issue on
in, in this prob, problem.

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:18.530
Professor Uwe Reinhardt of Princeton
described it as an ugly patch

00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:23.770
on an ugly system, that's, referred as a
complexity and so forth.

00:24:23.770 --> 00:24:27.830
And I think it's too bad that they didn't
break the link to employer-sponsored

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:33.910
insurance, and go for a simplified, really
universal health insurance.

00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:37.840
Which, for example, in the late, you know,
latter part of the,

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:43.170
last decade, Senator Wyden from Oregon and
Senator Bennett from Utah purposed, as did

00:24:43.170 --> 00:24:48.440
the comity for economic development, there
are ways you could do it.

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:52.000
I mean there's just a lot wrong with it
adds to the cost and

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:55.310
complexity, etc, of employer based health
insurance.

00:24:55.310 --> 00:24:56.520
We're all used to it.

00:24:56.520 --> 00:25:01.470
But it is that the, at the heart of, of,
why we have these problems.

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:05.270
So, there we are.

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:09.499
Thank you for your time and interest and

00:25:09.499 --> 00:25:15.118
good, good luck with your affordable
health insurance.

00:25:15.118 --> 00:25:17.611
[LAUGH] Questions?

00:25:17.611 --> 00:25:21.048
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:25:21.048 --> 00:25:22.160
They, they wanna give you a mic.

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:25.950
Yeah.

00:25:25.950 --> 00:25:26.670
Right.

00:25:26.670 --> 00:25:29.950
&gt;&gt; Yes Medicare when it first was passed
wasn't perfect either.

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:32.820
And there have been a lot of changes that
made it much better over the years.

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:37.850
And how can we change the Affordable Care
Act to make it better too?

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:39.970
what, what would you suggest.

00:25:39.970 --> 00:25:41.340
&gt;&gt; Well.
&gt;&gt; For single payer or like,

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:42.700
Medicare, or do we do something else?

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:50.680
&gt;&gt; Well I think first of all, with the
subsidies, they are means tested.

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:51.700
You know?
So that the,

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:55.380
the exchange has to go to the IRS to get
last years tax reported.

00:25:56.420 --> 00:25:57.435
It's a, and.

00:25:57.435 --> 00:25:58.054
&gt;&gt; [COUGH].

00:25:59.310 --> 00:26:02.070
&gt;&gt; Taxable income reported and so forth.

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:06.740
So, I would say the subsidy payments or
the premium support payments

00:26:07.850 --> 00:26:13.820
ought to apply on a flat basis across the
income spectrum and

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:20.440
not be means tested at that level except
for, if you, if you want means testing,

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:25.440
which I think is okay, then you would say
these premium support payments

00:26:26.710 --> 00:26:30.880
would be reported to the IRS and included
in the recipient's taxable income.

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:34.700
So that would reduce some of the cost,
cost to the government.

00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:42.130
But that would be one, one major thing.

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:45.367
Let me just say, if you say what do I
think we ought to have,

00:26:45.367 --> 00:26:51.560
[LAUGH] depressingly it has been 30 or so
years since I proposed,

00:26:52.810 --> 00:26:57.220
published in the New England Journal of
Medicine, consumer choice health plan.

00:26:57.220 --> 00:27:01.100
And the idea was, in effect, universal
health insurance based

00:27:01.100 --> 00:27:04.310
on regulated competition in the private
sector.

00:27:04.310 --> 00:27:08.020
So everybody, the whole country, oughtta
look like what we do at Stanford.

00:27:09.110 --> 00:27:15.166
Which is, we offer, our employees a range
of choices where

00:27:15.166 --> 00:27:21.802
they are responsible for the costs above
the cost of the low cost plan.

00:27:21.802 --> 00:27:27.541
And what happens, is, people will migrate
to what they see as value for money.

00:27:27.541 --> 00:27:28.483
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:27:28.483 --> 00:27:30.357
In the, in the interest of transparency,

00:27:30.357 --> 00:27:32.446
I have to reveal that I'm a retired
physician.

00:27:32.446 --> 00:27:35.784
And I'm currently, now, I am a hospital
medical administrator.

00:27:35.784 --> 00:27:37.685
So I've, we've dealt with some of these
things.

00:27:37.685 --> 00:27:41.209
One of the problems, even, with the, with
the exchanges, and

00:27:41.209 --> 00:27:45.667
some of the plans, is that people tend to,
sometimes, pick the cheapest plan.

00:27:45.667 --> 00:27:48.876
The bronze plan was basically have a 40%
deductible and

00:27:48.876 --> 00:27:52.869
I had to deal with a woman the other day,
came to our emergency room who had

00:27:52.869 --> 00:27:57.355
a $5,000 bill and here she had $2,000 that
she was responsible for.

00:27:57.355 --> 00:28:01.420
Now she didn't have very much money, she
complained, oh I thought I had insurance.

00:28:01.420 --> 00:28:05.120
You know, that, that seems to be, it's a
kind of a trap for many people.

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:05.988
They think they're insured.

00:28:05.988 --> 00:28:06.610
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:08.250
&gt;&gt; But they actually aren't completely
insured.

00:28:08.250 --> 00:28:09.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
That's a very good point.

00:28:09.190 --> 00:28:12.560
I wish I had included that in my prepared
remarks.

00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:15.520
Some of this is pretty lousy insurance,
you know, with very high deductibles.

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:21.770
And, for a lot of people, they're just not
gonna be able to afford,

00:28:21.770 --> 00:28:23.880
the, of the deductible.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:26.348
So, I think what happens there, is,

00:28:26.348 --> 00:28:31.518
there are some people in the political
world, for whom it's more important for

00:28:31.518 --> 00:28:36.477
us to be able to say they're insured, then
for them actually to be insured.

00:28:36.477 --> 00:28:39.932
In my consumer choice health plan
proposal,

00:28:39.932 --> 00:28:45.953
there'd be a standard coverage contract
and there'd be much less deductibles.

00:28:45.953 --> 00:28:49.391
The cost consciousness would come in the
premium.

00:28:49.391 --> 00:28:50.608
Yes?

00:28:50.608 --> 00:28:51.659
&gt;&gt; question.

00:28:51.659 --> 00:28:52.927
&gt;&gt; Was there any estimate made?

00:28:52.927 --> 00:28:56.091
&gt;&gt; Pardon me?
&gt;&gt; How much it's costing the dot comer,

00:28:56.091 --> 00:28:57.932
to 20 million people?

00:28:57.932 --> 00:29:00.000
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:00.534
[SOUND].

00:29:00.534 --> 00:29:01.335
&gt;&gt; How much-

00:29:01.335 --> 00:29:05.767
&gt;&gt; Has there been any estimate made of how
much it's costing the nation not to

00:29:05.767 --> 00:29:08.300
cover the 20 million people.

00:29:08.300 --> 00:29:13.190
&gt;&gt; I expect there probably have been.

00:29:13.190 --> 00:29:18.920
One doesn't come to mind immediately, but
that's a good question.

00:29:20.720 --> 00:29:24.210
Because those people well, I'll tell you
just an example,

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:29.557
I was talking with Senator Bob Bennett who
was

00:29:29.557 --> 00:29:34.070
senator from, Utah and I thought a very
good guy, but

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:41.250
fairly conservative, but more conservative
people in Utah fired him.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:44.600
And I remember he was talking to me about
this plan that he and

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:46.290
Senator Wyden were proposing.

00:29:46.290 --> 00:29:51.659
He said, you know, I say to people, we
already have universal health insurance.

00:29:51.659 --> 00:29:54.829
I mean, you can go to the hospital if
you're sick, and be cared for free.

00:29:54.829 --> 00:29:56.885
But the problem with that is.

00:29:56.885 --> 00:29:58.532
That's a very expensive way of doing it.

00:29:58.532 --> 00:30:01.960
Because those people should have had
access to primary care doctor who

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:04.397
would catch their problem early and treat
them and

00:30:04.397 --> 00:30:06.620
keep, keep them from needing the hospital.

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:15.150
And so I think there is a real cost, to
having people without health insurance.

00:30:15.150 --> 00:30:23.310
But it comes in, in disability and work
days lost school days lost you know, etc.

00:30:23.310 --> 00:30:27.230
And it'd be a good project to try to add
that all up.

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:30.020
Yes.

00:30:34.740 --> 00:30:37.850
&gt;&gt; So do you think or, how far are we from
a,

00:30:37.850 --> 00:30:44.080
a truly universal health insurance similar
to what's in U.K. or Canada?

00:30:44.080 --> 00:30:50.760
&gt;&gt; Well, we are at least 30 million people
away from that.

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:54.530
I mean that, you know, it's, it's somewhat
uncertain, there are varying estimates.

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:56.810
I just picked that as what the
congressional budget office

00:30:57.820 --> 00:30:59.020
said some time ago.

00:31:00.352 --> 00:31:04.210
The number will vary, there are many
states with

00:31:04.210 --> 00:31:08.587
Republican governors who decided that
they're not going to

00:31:08.587 --> 00:31:14.012
expand Medicaid up to everybody at 1.3
times the federal poverty line.

00:31:14.012 --> 00:31:19.102
And so, the score would be better if they,
as some states are doing now,

00:31:19.102 --> 00:31:24.778
negotiate with the government saying well,
we are willing to expand coverage,

00:31:24.778 --> 00:31:28.960
but we wanna do it in a more market
oriented way or whatever.

00:31:30.810 --> 00:31:33.720
So it's a, it's an uncertain and variable
number.

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:38.866
But take 30 million as an approximation.

00:31:38.866 --> 00:31:40.256
Yes.

00:31:40.256 --> 00:31:43.001
Back here.

00:31:43.001 --> 00:31:48.509
&gt;&gt; How does your, I think you called it
the Community Health Plan and

00:31:48.509 --> 00:31:53.716
or the Widen Bennett Plan, break the
linkage to employment or

00:31:53.716 --> 00:31:57.740
not and how do these plans address free
riding?

00:31:57.740 --> 00:32:02.551
&gt;&gt; Well, let's see, they, in that model,

00:32:02.551 --> 00:32:08.309
your insurance would not be linked to
employment.

00:32:10.190 --> 00:32:17.670
In fact I even what's wrong with employer
sponsored health insurance?

00:32:17.670 --> 00:32:21.500
It locks most people into open-ended fee
for service because employers don't have

00:32:21.500 --> 00:32:24.740
a very good re, record of, of offering
choices.

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:27.529
Means you lose your job, if you lose your
job you lose your insurance.

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:31.590
Employers use it to compete for

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:36.640
employees by making their, the insurance
ever more generous.

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:37.830
You know, we pay for everything.

00:32:37.830 --> 00:32:43.220
Come to work for us and we pay for
everything, etc.

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:47.390
including, nobody understands their health
insurances.

00:32:47.390 --> 00:32:49.270
Probably if I asked for a show of hands
here and

00:32:49.270 --> 00:32:53.325
said, how many of you, have actually read
your health insurance contract.

00:32:53.325 --> 00:32:56.852
[LAUGH] Actually read it.

00:32:56.852 --> 00:32:59.180
[LAUGH] Take a show of hands.

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:02.525
And iff somebody raise their hand I say,
did you understand it?

00:33:02.525 --> 00:33:07.620
[LAUGH] So, a consumer choice health plan
I

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:12.980
had in mind is relatively simple standard
definition of the insurance contract,

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:16.790
it wouldn't include a very high
deductible.

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:19.550
eh, but you wouldn't look to the, your
employer anymore.

00:33:19.550 --> 00:33:26.210
You would look to the, the exchange where
you, you'd get a subsidy.

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:28.390
Now, what I proposed was that,

00:33:28.390 --> 00:33:33.530
like Stanford your, the low-price plan
would be paid for.

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:37.370
And if you wanted something more
expensive,

00:33:37.370 --> 00:33:41.714
then you'd have to pay the difference, and
that would bring market forces to bear.

00:33:41.714 --> 00:33:46.280
And Wyden-Bennett a, a similar idea.

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:50.180
now, what about free riders?

00:33:50.180 --> 00:33:52.130
Well, the Affordable Care Act does have

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:57.940
what economists refer to as a tax on free
riders.

00:33:57.940 --> 00:34:02.250
So if you don't, you could have health
insurance free, mister.

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:08.740
And if you don't, then you know, you'll,
you'll have to pay this tax.

00:34:08.740 --> 00:34:16.310
Now I, I do think that the free rider tax
on the Affordable Care Act is,

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:21.450
is gonna be problematic and difficult to
collect and enforce and so forth.

00:34:21.450 --> 00:34:27.019
But, that, that, that's the best answer
out there.

00:34:27.019 --> 00:34:30.389
&gt;&gt; Dr.Antoven I, I have the microphone,
[INAUDIBLE].

00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:31.320
&gt;&gt; Oh, okay.

00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:32.500
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
Great talk.

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:34.240
I'm I'm married to an MBA.

00:34:34.240 --> 00:34:38.530
I'm a physician, and I have a question
that's been talked about in

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:41.640
the doctors lounges and in the boardrooms
of hospitals.

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:46.934
You so casually referred to the 30-day
readmission rate as

00:34:46.934 --> 00:34:49.190
a marker of care somehow.

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:52.530
And a, are you aware, or do you have a
comment on,

00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:57.120
that we have no control over the
noncompliant patient, the mentally ill

00:34:57.120 --> 00:35:02.010
patients, and those with chronic diseases,
who leave against medical advice.

00:35:02.010 --> 00:35:03.430
Or they bounce out,

00:35:03.430 --> 00:35:06.240
don't take their medicines, and they come
back within 30 days.

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:09.440
And we care so much about those patients,
but

00:35:09.440 --> 00:35:14.275
we have no control over their behavior
once they leave.

00:35:14.275 --> 00:35:16.950
&gt;&gt; Well, it is a challenge, and

00:35:16.950 --> 00:35:21.290
I just think [LAUGH], and I think you have
to work on it.

00:35:21.290 --> 00:35:22.640
The problem.

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:26.258
The problem comes from, I think, a large
part,

00:35:26.258 --> 00:35:34.157
that when the patient leaves the hospital
they ought to be part of the delivery

00:35:34.157 --> 00:35:39.680
system that right away picks them up and
puts them into the ambulatory care system.

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:45.660
And works with them to make sure that they
have their medications,

00:35:45.660 --> 00:35:51.730
and that they take their meds and, and you
know, do whatever they

00:35:51.730 --> 00:35:56.360
can instead of, you know, in the hospital,
I've experienced like,

00:35:56.360 --> 00:36:01.610
like wife has baby and in a wheel chair,
we take her to the hospital door.

00:36:01.610 --> 00:36:06.272
And the car pulls up, and they put him in,
and then, [SOUND] you know, goodbye.

00:36:06.272 --> 00:36:10.075
[LAUGH] With, without a lot of careful
preparation for

00:36:10.075 --> 00:36:14.380
what's supposed to happen after they leave
the hospital.

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:19.330
It's there are gonna be some 30 day,
within 30 day readmissions for

00:36:19.330 --> 00:36:25.240
the many reasons that you [COUGH]
enumerated.

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:32.909
But it's important to work on those post
hospitalization problems.

00:36:32.909 --> 00:36:35.382
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:36:35.382 --> 00:36:38.297
Yes?

00:36:38.297 --> 00:36:43.393
&gt;&gt; How many, how many years before it
becomes evident to

00:36:43.393 --> 00:36:48.037
Congress that there's a, a big flaw in the
act,

00:36:48.037 --> 00:36:54.380
whether it's cost containment or some
other portion of the act and

00:36:54.380 --> 00:37:00.293
Congress has to do something new, and then
what will they do?

00:37:00.293 --> 00:37:05.787
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] well, one thing is, one thing
is

00:37:05.787 --> 00:37:11.147
Obama and the Democrats have no shortage
of

00:37:11.147 --> 00:37:16.651
critics who are pointing out the various
flaws.

00:37:16.651 --> 00:37:20.980
And so it's hard to pick up a newspaper
without reading about one or

00:37:20.980 --> 00:37:26.330
another of the flaws in the Affordable
Care Act and I, I've mentioned a few,.

00:37:28.780 --> 00:37:36.820
When, when, whether and when the Congress
will act to fix them, it, beats me.

00:37:38.170 --> 00:37:43.710
But, I would say the direction they ought
to go is to make it more universal,

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:44.610
more cost contained.

00:37:44.610 --> 00:37:50.750
One of my big complaints about about the
Affordable Care Act is

00:37:52.010 --> 00:37:57.130
I think nobody should just be offered
open-ended fee for service fully paid.

00:37:57.130 --> 00:38:00.970
I think, like Stanford employees, you,

00:38:00.970 --> 00:38:05.030
you outta have a subsidy that pays a fixed
dollar amount, pays your

00:38:05.030 --> 00:38:10.540
way into a good quality integrated
delivery system or whatever is out there.

00:38:10.540 --> 00:38:14.500
And then, you have an incentive to choose
the economical system.

00:38:16.530 --> 00:38:18.960
And will we ever get there?

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.030
I don't know.
You know, we might,

00:38:21.030 --> 00:38:22.346
probably not in my lifetime.

00:38:22.346 --> 00:38:24.204
Dr. Beethoven?

00:38:24.204 --> 00:38:24.778
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:38:24.778 --> 00:38:26.630
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:26.630 --> 00:38:27.130
&gt;&gt; I,

00:38:27.130 --> 00:38:28.010
&gt;&gt; Oh, okay.
&gt;&gt; Thank you for

00:38:28.010 --> 00:38:29.940
your decades of wonderful work in this
area,

00:38:29.940 --> 00:38:31.850
I'm delighted to hear you today.

00:38:31.850 --> 00:38:34.990
I'm an OB GYN physician.
I just wanna try to encourage you

00:38:34.990 --> 00:38:38.920
to open your mind a little more about the
reproductive health issues.

00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:41.410
I see women in those decisions.

00:38:41.410 --> 00:38:45.590
And, you know, in America we are free from
religious persecution,

00:38:45.590 --> 00:38:47.890
that's how America was founded.

00:38:47.890 --> 00:38:51.980
And I think that if we agree that women
deserve reproductive health,

00:38:53.430 --> 00:38:54.880
we've agreed that women deserve that, and

00:38:54.880 --> 00:38:57.510
that needs to be apart of our health
insurance regardless of

00:38:57.510 --> 00:39:00.710
religious organizations having their own
personal points of view.

00:39:00.710 --> 00:39:04.231
So I encourage you to change your thinking
on that.

00:39:04.231 --> 00:39:10.340
&gt;&gt; Well, one good thing about, [APPLAUSE]
one good thing about about breaking

00:39:10.340 --> 00:39:16.560
the link between employment and health
insurance, I, I probably should have.

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:20.750
Next time I do this, I'm gonna put that on
the slide.

00:39:20.750 --> 00:39:25.310
Is then, the employer is not involved in
the issue of the contents of your health

00:39:25.310 --> 00:39:30.660
insurance, and I just don't think it's,
that it's a necessary or

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:33.820
appropriate thing to have the employer
involved in that.

00:39:33.820 --> 00:39:38.920
It ought to be just you know, out there on
its own merits.

00:39:40.090 --> 00:39:44.810
So yeah, that, that,

00:39:44.810 --> 00:39:49.770
that would be one, it's, it's just, seemed
to me it was unnecessarily seeking or

00:39:49.770 --> 00:39:55.039
bringing about conflict that didn't need
to occur.

00:39:55.039 --> 00:39:56.705
Yes?

00:39:56.705 --> 00:40:03.902
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:03.902 --> 00:40:08.437
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Gotta get the mic.

00:40:08.437 --> 00:40:09.470
Here it comes.

00:40:09.470 --> 00:40:14.333
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:40:14.333 --> 00:40:16.750
&gt;&gt; My bad.

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:22.080
I'm puzzled by the the difference in
billing between what

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:26.570
the insurance company pays, and what,
there's a number that's like four,

00:40:26.570 --> 00:40:31.380
five times as high that appears on the
bill which as,

00:40:31.380 --> 00:40:34.856
as far as I can tell, applies to anybody
who can't afford to pay at all.

00:40:34.856 --> 00:40:40.140
[LAUGH] And they're the only ones that
have to pay that number.

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:42.000
That, that doesn't make any sense to me.

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:43.120
And it's true of hospitals.

00:40:43.120 --> 00:40:45.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Is that true of me doctors, et cetera?

00:40:45.950 --> 00:40:47.200
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:40:47.200 --> 00:40:48.420
It doesn't make any sense.

00:40:48.420 --> 00:40:48.920
It's crazy.

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:54.200
Let me explain where I, you know, how
could this thing happen?

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:59.420
It turns out that hospitals have a very

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:03.800
substantial number of patients who got
there through the emergency room.

00:41:05.480 --> 00:41:10.727
And, if this is a hospital that is not in
the network of the insurance

00:41:10.727 --> 00:41:16.360
company covering the patient, then the
poor guy is, is at their mercy.

00:41:16.360 --> 00:41:19.705
You know, they can just and that they,
they make a lot of money that way.

00:41:19.705 --> 00:41:22.150
Cuz, people say, well why do they do that?

00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:23.080
And nobody pays that.

00:41:23.080 --> 00:41:24.290
Well.
Some people do pay it, and

00:41:24.290 --> 00:41:32.650
support people who are there as a result
of an emergency, or urgent situation.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:34.983
And, and I think that that's wrong.

00:41:34.983 --> 00:41:38.749
I think I would favor some kind of a law
that would say,

00:41:38.749 --> 00:41:41.594
you can't charge more than, you know,

00:41:41.594 --> 00:41:47.280
something like 1.5 times Medicare or, your
know, some reasonable limit.

00:41:47.280 --> 00:41:52.220
Otherwise it's just it's just extorting
people from, you know, it's taking

00:41:55.530 --> 00:42:00.890
advantage of the fact that, that people
are, are sick and it.

00:42:00.890 --> 00:42:08.940
Especially for a non-profit institution I
think it's particularly in,

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:13.010
you know, inappropriate and wrong.

00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:19.781
Maybe this, maybe the thing to do, you
know, people have made the point that

00:42:19.781 --> 00:42:26.830
hospitals engage in a lot of business that
for profit companies do also.

00:42:26.830 --> 00:42:28.400
So, the for

00:42:28.400 --> 00:42:34.670
profit companies refer to the non profit
hospitals as not tax paying hospitals.

00:42:34.670 --> 00:42:36.928
We're investor owned tax paying hospitals.

00:42:36.928 --> 00:42:40.460
Well, so that, that's been an issue.

00:42:40.460 --> 00:42:44.890
Perhaps the way to get at it would be to
say, to change the law to say if you

00:42:44.890 --> 00:42:50.880
are a non profit hospital then you will
not charge emergency

00:42:50.880 --> 00:42:55.540
patients more than 1.5 times Medicare, or
something like that.

00:42:57.670 --> 00:42:59.180
Oh, wanna give him the mic?

00:42:59.180 --> 00:43:01.840
Oh.
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:03.720
&gt;&gt; Oh, somebody back there.

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:04.720
&gt;&gt; Professor Antoven.

00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:09.546
Some have suggested that, due to the
intransigence of Congress,

00:43:09.546 --> 00:43:13.339
the divisiveness, that the inevitable
outcome for

00:43:13.339 --> 00:43:17.218
the country is a Canada style socialized
medicine for

00:43:17.218 --> 00:43:22.758
the masses, has a separate tier of premium
care for those who can afford it.

00:43:22.758 --> 00:43:24.139
Do you think this is where we're heading?

00:43:24.139 --> 00:43:27.576
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:43:27.576 --> 00:43:30.118
&gt;&gt; [BLANK_AUDIO]

00:43:30.118 --> 00:43:32.886
You know, I, I, I don't know.

00:43:32.886 --> 00:43:37.338
I think the Canadian system works pretty
well for Canadians, but I,

00:43:37.338 --> 00:43:39.580
I don't think it would work here.

00:43:40.660 --> 00:43:46.640
I, I don't see that out there.

00:43:46.640 --> 00:43:49.130
It's, it's a good question.

00:43:51.000 --> 00:43:56.741
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:43:56.741 --> 00:43:58.615
Will congress act on something?

00:43:58.615 --> 00:44:04.672
[LAUGH] Oh, only reluctantly and in a
complex and confused way.

00:44:04.672 --> 00:44:09.830
[LAUGH]
Dr.Burnett?

00:44:09.830 --> 00:44:11.873
&gt;&gt; The recent,
&gt;&gt; This is Dr.Burnett, my friend.

00:44:11.873 --> 00:44:13.180
[LAUGH].

00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:18.516
&gt;&gt; Recent study by University of
California showed that hospital led,

00:44:18.516 --> 00:44:25.048
healthcare delivery systems controlled by
physicians were at least 20% cheaper

00:44:25.048 --> 00:44:32.550
than hospitals that didn't, medical
delivery systems controlled by hospitals.

00:44:32.550 --> 00:44:39.153
And the, there are many ways and positions
even in the beeper

00:44:39.153 --> 00:44:45.052
service system, can be peer reviewed and
under control.

00:44:45.052 --> 00:44:50.357
And have some future limitations on costs
there,

00:44:50.357 --> 00:44:53.761
I don't see any in hospitals, and I.

00:44:53.761 --> 00:44:56.683
Think about the affordable care act.

00:44:56.683 --> 00:45:00.190
And having the group of hospitals and
physicians.

00:45:00.190 --> 00:45:03.590
It's still going to be dominated by the
ones that have the capital

00:45:03.590 --> 00:45:04.210
which are the hospitals.

00:45:04.210 --> 00:45:05.442
There fearing,

00:45:05.442 --> 00:45:10.811
there's not lots of hospitals per
population in many areas of country.

00:45:10.811 --> 00:45:17.154
And how do you, what do you think is a
solution, to make this more efficient

00:45:17.154 --> 00:45:22.972
a system from the hospital side, not just
from the medical side?

00:45:22.972 --> 00:45:24.684
&gt;&gt; That's a very good question.

00:45:24.684 --> 00:45:28.853
I'd like to say, Doctor Burnett has been a
pioneer in this area.

00:45:28.853 --> 00:45:33.537
In phys, he's a physician leader, in
physicians getting organized to

00:45:33.537 --> 00:45:37.548
bring down the cost, and improve the
quality, and so forth.

00:45:37.548 --> 00:45:40.570
Bob, I just read that article yesterday.

00:45:40.570 --> 00:45:44.590
The author, Jamie Robinson, is a good
friend of mine, and I could, and through

00:45:44.590 --> 00:45:49.820
the Integrated Healthcare Association I've
kind of seen that research progress.

00:45:49.820 --> 00:45:55.833
And what had happens is the hospital
owned.

00:45:55.833 --> 00:46:02.290
Medical groups cost 1.41 times the
non-hospital.

00:46:02.290 --> 00:46:06.540
It's a very remark, marked difference,
now, my reaction to that was,

00:46:06.540 --> 00:46:09.480
well you know, the problem is with
hospital administrators,

00:46:09.480 --> 00:46:13.270
their whole orientation has been heads and
beds.

00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:16.170
Keep the, keep the hospital full to, to be
able to.

00:46:17.480 --> 00:46:19.900
Pay the overhead costs which is usually
large.

00:46:19.900 --> 00:46:22.070
And keep the CT Scanners, scanning.

00:46:22.070 --> 00:46:26.810
And keep all that stuff to bring in
revenue and

00:46:26.810 --> 00:46:32.310
the whole orientation of the efficient
delivery system, is lets do everything

00:46:32.310 --> 00:46:36.410
we can to keep the people healthy and keep
them from leaving the hospital.

00:46:36.410 --> 00:46:38.690
And see if we can empty those hospitals
beds.

00:46:38.690 --> 00:46:42.450
It's kind of a completely different
orientation and mindset.

00:46:42.450 --> 00:46:47.793
And so, I don't think that hospital
administrators have the right

00:46:47.793 --> 00:46:53.525
to mindset an ethos to be leaders in in
the healthcare systems.

00:46:53.525 --> 00:46:57.066
And it, it ought to be physicians.

00:46:57.066 --> 00:46:58.469
Like, like you and

00:46:58.469 --> 00:47:03.750
your colleagues who pioneered in creating
physician organizations.

00:47:05.152 --> 00:47:08.662
Over here.

00:47:08.662 --> 00:47:09.275
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:47:09.275 --> 00:47:11.007
My question's sort of a follow up.

00:47:11.007 --> 00:47:13.584
But with a little different spin.

00:47:13.584 --> 00:47:15.400
The United States has far and

00:47:15.400 --> 00:47:19.640
away the most expensive care per capita of
any developed country.

00:47:19.640 --> 00:47:22.720
And we have the worse outcomes on average.

00:47:22.720 --> 00:47:25.110
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; And all those other countries,

00:47:25.110 --> 00:47:26.050
where they spend less money and

00:47:26.050 --> 00:47:33.600
they get better outcomes basically are
single payer systems.

00:47:33.600 --> 00:47:38.590
And for the life of me, I cannot
understand why there isn't more serious,

00:47:38.590 --> 00:47:43.570
you know, consideration of a single-payer
system in a capitalist country where we

00:47:43.570 --> 00:47:52.090
should be concerned about keeping the cost
and getting better outcomes.

00:47:52.090 --> 00:47:56.336
&gt;&gt; Well, I guess it's partly a problem
that I, I have an attitude which was.

00:47:56.336 --> 00:48:02.432
Developed, I, I spent four years as an
assistant secretary of defense and

00:48:02.432 --> 00:48:08.935
my job was to, to bring cost effectiveness
into defense decision making.

00:48:08.935 --> 00:48:12.009
And it's quixotic task for a young man.

00:48:12.009 --> 00:48:14.333
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:48:14.333 --> 00:48:19.517
&gt;&gt; What I learned was things like the
ideal weapon system is built in

00:48:19.517 --> 00:48:23.374
435 district, Congressional districts.

00:48:23.374 --> 00:48:25.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You know.

00:48:25.060 --> 00:48:29.380
And, and if I look at Medicare, the pork
barrel,

00:48:29.380 --> 00:48:35.820
the log-rolling etcetera, I just don't see
any.

00:48:35.820 --> 00:48:38.000
I don't wanna sound like a right wing
fanatic, but

00:48:38.000 --> 00:48:42.620
this is, I was listening to democratic
administration that I learned this.

00:48:42.620 --> 00:48:47.900
It's just there are not enough people
seriously interested in the total, and

00:48:47.900 --> 00:48:49.900
holding down the total cost.

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:54.461
And, and well let me come at it another
way.

00:48:55.889 --> 00:49:04.699
I think it was justice Brandeis who said,
that our system of government was based,

00:49:04.699 --> 00:49:10.820
was built, to prevent the arbitrary
exercise of power.

00:49:10.820 --> 00:49:16.880
You know, so you think of Madison, and
ambition must be made to check ambition.

00:49:16.880 --> 00:49:22.030
So that, so nothing could get done In
contrast to the parliamentary system,

00:49:22.030 --> 00:49:28.307
which is a system that's very good for
managing public programs.

00:49:28.307 --> 00:49:32.840
So Brandeis we'are saying, our system of
governing is not built for the management,

00:49:32.840 --> 00:49:34.440
effective management of public programs.

00:49:34.440 --> 00:49:38.446
A huge difference between the
Parliamentary system and

00:49:38.446 --> 00:49:42.840
the system of checks and balances that we
have.

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:43.540
Just to go on for

00:49:43.540 --> 00:49:46.800
a moment, I've done some work on the
British National Health Service

00:49:48.040 --> 00:49:50.090
at the invitation of the British people to
come over.

00:49:50.090 --> 00:49:53.100
And, and they've got their problems,

00:49:53.100 --> 00:49:58.050
I grant you for half the share of GDP,
they get better outcomes.

00:49:58.050 --> 00:50:02.070
I think for some, some interesting
reasons.

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:08.660
And anyway, so I was asked to, how, how
could we bring some market forces and

00:50:08.660 --> 00:50:13.270
competition into the business national
health service?

00:50:14.660 --> 00:50:15.840
So there's a stature.

00:50:17.290 --> 00:50:20.280
Picked up on my ideas and tried to
implement them.

00:50:20.280 --> 00:50:21.690
And so the doctors didn't like it.

00:50:21.690 --> 00:50:25.880
And I was invited to lecture to the London
medical society.

00:50:25.880 --> 00:50:29.670
And this doctor got up and said we live.

00:50:29.670 --> 00:50:32.160
We have an elective dictatorship.

00:50:32.160 --> 00:50:34.805
She can do whatever she wants and there's
nothing we can do about it.

00:50:34.805 --> 00:50:40.780
[LAUGH] And so I said in reply, well,

00:50:40.780 --> 00:50:45.841
that's all very well, but I come from a
country that doesn't have any government.

00:50:45.841 --> 00:50:49.310
[LAUGH] It's all, all checks and

00:50:49.310 --> 00:50:54.960
balances and so I, I just don't think
it's, our form of government is built for.

00:50:54.960 --> 00:50:56.380
The affect of management of public
programs,

00:50:56.380 --> 00:50:57.230
we can see that all over the place.

00:50:57.230 --> 00:50:58.970
&gt;&gt; I mean, my husband and I just went on
Medicare not that long ago.

00:50:58.970 --> 00:51:00.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:51:00.010 --> 00:51:04.686
&gt;&gt; That is a kind of a single care system.

00:51:04.686 --> 00:51:07.240
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:51:07.240 --> 00:51:13.630
&gt;&gt; I wonder how

00:51:13.630 --> 00:51:15.390
many people in this auditorium on Medicare
would wanna get rid of it?

00:51:15.390 --> 00:51:15.990
&gt;&gt; Well I think it outta be-

00:51:15.990 --> 00:51:16.535
&gt;&gt; You know, it's a single-
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:51:16.535 --> 00:51:18.460
[INAUDIBLE]

00:51:18.460 --> 00:51:24.400
&gt;&gt; I, I, I think Medicare ought to be
phased into a new model,

00:51:24.400 --> 00:51:27.300
which it, resembles what we do at Stanford
for our employees.

00:51:27.300 --> 00:51:32.890
That is, the government, by the way,
several bipartisan commissions and

00:51:32.890 --> 00:51:34.920
groups have recommended that we do this.

